# shir_nodes/fs_newer_than.node — the `-nt` / `-ot` file predicates.
#
# Bash/GNU define these with a MISSING-OPERAND rule that is easy to get wrong:
#   A -nt B  is true iff A exists and (B does not exist OR mtime(A) > mtime(B))
#   A -ot B  is true iff B exists and (A does not exist OR mtime(A) < mtime(B))
# so exactly one of the two is true for a pair of missing/existing files, and
# neither is true when BOTH are missing.
#
# The comparison is over the FULL timestamp (nanoseconds on a modern fs). A
# renderer that compares seconds turns a pair written in the same second into
# "not newer" when bash says newer — a silently wrong answer, and the reason
# this node exists instead of a `__sh_mtime() > __sh_mtime()` spelling.
#
# `newer = true` is `-nt`, `newer = false` is `-ot`; the missing-operand rule
# is part of the node, not the renderer.
node FsNewerThan
tag "FsNewerThan"
kind expr
field left: expr
field right: expr
field newer: bool
spec: FsSemantics#newer_than
